So, the number of years she needs to work more is $40 - y$.
Since the question implies we don't know how long she has worked so far, we are looking for the number of additional years she needs to work.
Let the number of years she has worked be $y$. The total number of years she will work is $y+x = y+40$.
The number of additional years she needs to work is $40-y$.
However, the question asks how many more years does Lauren need to work, so we are asking for $40-y$. Since $y$ is not given, we can assume that she hasn't worked any years yet, so $y=0$. Then the number of additional years she needs to work is $40-0=40$.

Let's consider a slightly different approach. She wants to save for 20 years. She saves 20% of her salary each year. So, in 20 years, she saves $20 \times 0.2 \times 100000 = 400000$.
