John buys 10 bottles of scotch for a total cost of $600. So, $10c_s = 600$. Therefore, $c_s = \frac{600}{10} = 60$. The cost of one bottle of scotch is $60.
He buys twice as many bottles of cognac as he buys bottles of scotch. So, he buys $2 \times 10 = 20$ bottles of cognac.
The cognac costs 50% more per bottle than the scotch. So, $c_c = c_s + 0.50 c_s = 1.50 c_s = 1.50 \times 60 = 90$. The cost of one bottle of cognac is $90.
The cost of 20 bottles of cognac is $20 \times c_c = 20 \times 90 = 1800$.
The total cost is the cost of scotch plus the cost of cognac.
Total cost = $600 + 1800 = 2400$.
